      "correct_answer": "A",
      "correct_answer_raw": "",
      "correct_answer_plain": "A",
      "correct_answer_images": [],
      "explanation": "The answer is A.\nThe genetic code is a triplet code (three bases code for one amino acid). A section of DNA with 420 base pairs can code for 140 amino acids (420 divided by 3).\nThe DNA contains four bases (adenine, guanine, cytosine and thymine). Adenine binds with thymine and guanine with cytosine. If the DNA contains 42% adenine it will also contain 42% thymine. 42% × 2 is 84%.\nThis means that 16% are guanine and cytosine together. 16% divided by 2 is 8%.\nThis DNA therefore contains 42% of adenine, 42% of thymine, 8% of cytosine and 8% of guanine.",
      "explanation_images": [],
      "extracted_at": "2026-07-05T00:11:59.801768",
